    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d648">

                <pb facs="0660" n="654" sameAs="frus:frus1942v02/pg654"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">851A.01/28: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in Canada (Moffat) to the Secretary of State
                    </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Ottawa</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1942-01-01T12:00:00-04:00">January 1,
                            1942—noon.</date>
                        <lb/> [Received 12:54 p.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>1. Reference various telephone conversations regarding status of St.
                    Pierre-Miquelon. The problems raised by Admiral Muselier’s<anchor n="16"
                        xml:id="d648-f1"/> occupancy of the islands of St. Pierre-Miquelon are under
                    continued discussion in the Canadian press. Although public sympathy has from
                    the beginning been with the Admiral there was a willingness to recognize that
                    his move might have such far flung repercussions on <pb facs="0661" n="655"
                        xml:id="pg655"/> North Africa, the French Fleet, hemispheric policies, etc.,
                    that some form of accommodation, which safeguarded wireless communications,
                    might be a wise move. Since Mr. Churchill’s speech before Parliament<anchor
                        n="17" xml:id="d648-f2"/> in which he excoriated Vichy’s plans and purposes,
                    public opinion has swung as a virtual unit against any form of attempted
                    compromise. Led by prominent members of Parliament and echoes by the press, Mr.
                        King<anchor n="18" xml:id="d648-f3"/> is being petitioned to take no part in
                    any policy that would savor of a rebuff to General de Gaulle.<anchor n="19"
                        xml:id="d648-f4"/> The unfortunate impression is gaining ground that there
                    is a rift in policy between London and Washington in the matter of dealing with
                    France and public opinion in its present mood is behind what it thinks is
                    London’s position.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Moffat</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d648-f1" n="16" xml:id="d648-f1-content">
                <p>Adm. Emile Muselier, Free French Minister of the Navy.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d648-f2" n="17" xml:id="d648-f2-content">
                <p>Speech of British Prime Minister Winston S. Churchill before the Canadian
                    Parliament, December 30, 1941.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d648-f3" n="18" xml:id="d648-f3-content">
                <p>W. L. Mackenzie King, Canadian Prime Minister.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d648-f4" n="19" xml:id="d648-f4-content">
                <p>Gen. Charles de Gaulle, President of the National Committee of the Free French at
                    London.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
